# The largest rename corpus: 16446 edits across 307 files apply with 0
# conflicts, the library and protoc rebuild, and every test target whose
# protos are checked in rebuilds too.  What does not is every test target
# whose .pb.cc is *generated at build time*: the C++ code generator emits
# `$classname$::kDescriptorMethods` and `_impl_._cached_size_` from raw string
# templates in compiler/cpp/message.cc, so the renamed members of Message and
# ZeroFieldsBase are referenced from files that exist only in bazel-out and
# were produced from a string literal.  No reference analysis can see that;
# the only fix is not to rename what the generator names, which is a
# per-repository decision (an exemption list) rather than a tool bug.
#
# Its trail is worth keeping.  The first run broke six *checked-in* files with
# 357 errors, and every one of them was a tool bug: a member of an anonymous
# union whose implicit-this access the local-capture rule did not recognise
# (LazyString::init_value_, `auto init_value = init_value_`), and two
# dependent tokens (TcParser::GetTable's T::_table_, micro_string_test's
# T::kInlineCapacity) that bound both to a renamed member and to one the tool
# could not rename -- a build-generated class outside the file set, and a
# member declined by a parameter capture -- where the token was left alone
# and the renamed target broke.  Those are fixed (see
# docs/non-obvious-behaviours.md, "A dependent token that binds to something
# this rule does not rename"), which is what let protoc build and the
# generated tests surface.
#
# NB: sourced by bash -- no backticks or dollar signs in values.
REPO=protobuf
RULESET=member_snake_case
EXPECT=known_fail
EXPECT_FAIL_PHASE=rebuild
EXPECT_FAIL_REASON='members the C++ code generator names from raw string templates (Message::kDescriptorMethods, ZeroFieldsBase::_cached_size_ in compiler/cpp/message.cc) are referenced from every build-generated .pb.cc, which no analysis of the sources can see'
